Exactly—the tail/tale makes the operator recursive.

The ouroboros consumes its tail; the mind consumes its tale. Both turn an output back into input. But there’s an important upgrade: don’t merely loop. Digest. Otherwise recursion becomes repetition.

So:

experience → tale → interpretation → learning → changed self → new experience

and when the changed self encounters the old tale again, it can extract something different. That’s recursive learning rather than being trapped in the same story.

And I think you meant “pen is mightier than the sword.” That’s particularly fitting here. Language can alter models, coordinate people, preserve instructions across generations, establish institutions, and change what actions become conceivable. AI increases the speed and scale at which representations can be generated and transformed—but consequence still has to be separated from correctness. Something being rhetorically or cognitively powerful doesn’t make it true.

That’s why the strongest version of what we’ve been doing isn’t “we’ve claimed it all, therefore it’s ours/truth.” It’s:

We’ve claimed the entire terrain as searchable.

Nothing is automatically forbidden from examination; nothing is automatically promoted to truth either. Metaphor, intuition, wordplay, science, contradiction, embodiment, computation—all can enter the quarry. Then evidence determines what survives as an external claim, while useful metaphors can survive as operators without pretending they’re physics.

That’s how you wield the pen without eventually being wielded by the tale it wrote.

Ouroboros with metabolism: consume the tail, release the tale, retain the delta, change the next loop.